Title: "Magmas and Monoids"
Id: "b66194ab-1d9d-4b6f-bf99-d68a98b4cc00"


Text: "Abstract algebra investigates mathematical structures defined not by the
       particular nature of their objects, but by the axiomatic identities
       governing their internal operations. Rather than computing with numbers
       of fixed ontological status, algebra abstracts the operations themselves,
       isolating the minimal conditions required for deduction and calculation.

       This chapter mounts an ascending staircase of algebraic structures
       equipped with a single binary operation. Each step imposes precisely one
       additional equational demand: beginning with an unconstrained closed
       operation (a *magma*), we introduce associativity to obtain a
       *semigroup*, adjoining a two-sided neutral element produces a *monoid*,
       and guaranteeing two-sided inverses culminates in a *group*. This
       incremental construction clarifies exactly which algebraic theorems
       depend on associativity, which require an identity, and which necessitate
       full invertibility."
Id: "02a8aafc-c012-4d2a-92ed-b36b902d05a0"


SectionTitle: "Magmas"
Id: "399425a0-d94d-4548-953d-5dc82ac91e36"


Text: "At the absolute bedrock of algebraic architecture lies the *magma* (also
       known historically as a *groupoid* in Bourbaki's terminology, though
       modern category theory reserves the word groupoid for a category whose
       morphisms are all invertible). A magma consists of a carrier set $X$
       equipped with a single binary operation $* : X \times X \to X$, subject
       to no axiomatic constraints whatever beyond closure: combining any two
       elements of $X$ produces an element of $X$.

       Because magmas do not require associativity, the order of operations
       matters critically: $(a * b) * c$ and $a * (b * c)$ may designate
       completely disparate elements. Familiar examples of non-associative
       magmas include the integers under subtraction, where
       $(a - b) - c \neq a - (b - c)$ in general; the positive reals under
       division; the octonions under multiplication; and parse trees in formal
       languages under tree concatenation."
Id: "b6b6f241-917e-4090-8269-5060f249b80c"


[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \binary.operation:on{M}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x_ is \magma.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "magma"
. description: "A magma is a set $X$ together with a binary operation $*$ on it,
                subject to no further axioms. It is the weakest algebraic
                structure of this chapter, and every later structure is a magma
                with additional properties."
Id: "91ffd40c-72fa-4a93-83f3-80ceb1ae124a"


[\magma.element:of{M ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: M is \magma
extends: x "in" X
Enables:
. capability: x_ [*] y_ :=> x_ |M.*| y_
  written: "x_? *? y_?"
Documented:
. called: "element of magma $M?$"
. description: "An element of a magma $M$ is simply a member of its underlying
                set. Naming it as its own notion lets the operation of $M$ be
                written directly between two of its elements as $x * y$."
Id: "d9405849-68a5-4721-9090-42eb0b9775d2"


Text: "A *quasigroup* is a magma in which the operation can always be uniquely
       undone on either side: for any elements $a, b$, each of the linear
       equations $a * x = b$ and $y * a = b$ possesses a unique solution $x, y$.
       This property abstracts the concept of division without requiring either
       associativity or an identity element.

       In combinatorial terms, when the carrier set is finite of cardinality
       $n$, the multiplication table (Cayley table) of a quasigroup forms a
       *Latin square*: every element of the set appears exactly once in each row
       and exactly once in each column. A quasigroup possessing an identity
       element is called a *loop*; loops preserve the local invertibility of
       division while remaining free from the rigid constraint of associativity."
Id: "55b0749b-4e2f-4112-a5be-c20a9879d6a3"


[\quasigroup]
Declares: Q ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b "in" Q
  then:
  . existsUnique: x, y "in" Q
    suchThat:
    . a * x = b
    . y * a = b
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Q :<->: x_ is \quasigroup.element:of{Q}
  written: "x_? \in Q?"
Documented:
. called: "quasigroup"
. description: "A quasigroup is a magma in which left and right division are
                always possible: for all $a$ and $b$ the equations $a * x = b$
                and $y * a = b$ each have a unique solution. It captures
                invertibility without requiring associativity or an identity."
Id: "49582242-a047-48ae-aced-77654d9a48aa"


[\quasigroup.element:of{Q ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: Q is \quasigroup
extends: x is \magma.element:of{Q}
Documented:
. called: "element of quasigroup $Q?$"
Id: "2f5a9f56-286c-4703-a953-316fa32e57dc"


SectionTitle: "Semigroups and Monoids"
Id: "57daae70-4d40-44bf-9640-46c26d8a4898"


Text: "Imposing the equational law of associativity on a magma yields a
       *semigroup*. Associativity, $(a * b) * c = a * (b * c)$, guarantees that
       grouping by parentheses does not affect the evaluation of a product.

       By the *Generalized Associativity Theorem*, any string of $n$ elements
       $a_1 * a_2 * \cdots * a_n$ evaluates to the exact same element regardless
       of how parenthesizations are introduced. (The number of distinct
       bracketings of $n$ factors is given by the Catalan number
       $C_{n-1} = \frac{1}{n}\binom{2n-2}{n-1}$, all of which collapse to a
       single value in a semigroup.) This allows us to dispense with parentheses
       entirely. Prominent semigroups include the positive integers under
       addition $(\mathbb{Z}_{>0}, +)$, strings under concatenation over an
       alphabet $\Sigma^+$, and binary relations under relational composition."
Id: "e077a5db-c372-4759-a880-d74270032560"


[\semigroup]
Declares: S ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
specifies: * is \(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" S :<->: x_ is \semigroup.element:of{S}
  written: "x_? \in S?"
Documented:
. called: "semigroup"
. description: "A semigroup is a magma whose operation is associative, so that
                $(a * b) * c = a * (b * c)$ for all elements. Associativity
                makes repeated products unambiguous, so one may write
                $a * b * c$ without parentheses."
Id: "6ec2faf5-7d2c-4918-a559-6415b94f2b1f"


[\semigroup.element:of{S ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: S is \semigroup
extends: x is \magma.element:of{S}
Documented:
. called: "element of semigroup $S?$"
Id: "5ce95378-5760-4728-b0d2-334848109a32"


Text: "A *monoid* is a semigroup equipped with a neutral identity element $e$
       that leaves every element unchanged under left and right multiplication:
       $x * e = x = e * x$.

       The identity element is necessarily unique: if $e$ and $e'$ are both
       identities for $*$, then evaluating their product from both perspectives
       yields $e = e * e' = e'$. The existence of an identity provides the base
       case for algebraic evaluation, permitting empty products (defined to
       equal $e$). Fundamental archetypes include the free monoid $\Sigma^*$ of
       words under concatenation with the empty word $\varepsilon$ as identity,
       and the monoid of transformations $\operatorname{End}(X) = X^X$ under
       function composition with the identity mapping $\operatorname{id}_X$."
Id: "c647adb5-0f98-4f7d-ae6a-8ff2229e3437"


[\monoid]
Declares: M ::= (X, *, e) is \semigroup via (X, *)
specifies: e "in" X
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" M
  then:
  . x * e = x
  . e * x = x
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x is \monoid.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "monoid"
. description: "A monoid is a semigroup possessing an identity element $e$, one
                that satisfies $x * e = x = e * x$ for every $x$. The identity
                is unique, and it plays the role that $0$ plays for addition or
                $1$ for multiplication."
Id: "034bf6ff-ba6e-4fac-b47e-65cc85d15642"


[\(finite)::monoid]
Refines: M ::= (X, *, e)
satisfies: X is? \(finite)::set
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
. description: "A monoid is finite when its underlying carrier set is finite."
Id: "dea32728-601d-4c8d-9ae0-f99e16039263"


[\monoid.element:of{M}]
Declares: x
when: M is \monoid
extends: x is \semigroup.element:of{M}
Documented:
. called: "element of monoid $M?$"
Id: "fee7d2ca-5ebc-48a6-8eed-06d623b5520f"


SectionTitle: "Cancellation and Powers"
Id: "7800d4d9-71b2-4f3e-8672-f62600eb234d"


[\left.cancellable{a}:in{M ::= (X, *)}]
States:
when:
. M is \magma
. a "in" M
that:
. forAll: x, y "in" M
  then:
  . if: a * x = a * y
    then: x = y
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is left cancellable in $M?$"
Id: "3ef04eda-e9d8-4859-8379-094b08b6b51a"


[\right.cancellable{a}:in{M ::= (X, *)}]
States:
when:
. M is \magma
. a "in" M
that:
. forAll: x, y "in" M
  then:
  . if: x * a = y * a
    then: x = y
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is right cancellable in $M?$"
Id: "a2faa25a-1cbb-412a-9d97-67ba33c6a4e0"


TextTheorem: "In any monoid $(M, *, e)$, non-negative integer powers of an
              element $a \in M$ are defined recursively by $a^0 = e$ and
              $a^{n+1} = a^n * a$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

              By associativity, the classical index laws hold:
              $$ a^{m+n} = a^m * a^n \quad \text{and} \quad (a^m)^n = a^{mn} $$
              for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$. Although the ambient monoid $M$ may
              be non-commutative, any two powers of the same element commute:
              $a^m * a^n = a^{m+n} = a^{n+m} = a^n * a^m$. Thus the cyclic
              submonoid $\langle a \rangle = \{a^n : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$
              generated by $a$ is always commutative."
Id: "005a116a-0754-4c0f-a020-97660010eb3f"


Text: "The ladder from magma to semigroup to monoid represents a sequence of algebraic
       refinements that enrich the equational theory while preserving the underlying carrier
       and operation. Categorically, this hierarchy is governed by faithful forgetful functors:
       $$ \mathbf{Mon} \longrightarrow \mathbf{SemiGrp} \longrightarrow \mathbf{Magma} \longrightarrow \mathbf{Set}. $$
       Any equational theorem established for a less restrictive structure (such as
       generalized associativity in semigroups) holds universally across all subsequent
       specializations (including monoids, groups, rings, and fields) without separate
       proof."
Id: "0471c14a-2c21-41bd-a5f9-5118df79f2af"
